79 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E ( Local JSON + Fetch API \(current implementation\): Bus routes, timings, bus details, and pass information are stored in simulated JSON files. The React application retrieves this information asynchronously using the Fetch API \(AJAX\) and stores it in React state. This keeps the data separate from the UI and makes the prototype easy to build and modify, but the data is simulated rather than live transport data.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
80 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (Bus Management API + Database \(planned\): React would communicate with a backend API connected to a college transport database. This would allow the application to work with actual bus schedules, routes, bus availability, student pass applications, and driver information. Authentication and transport-admin functionality could also be added in a real deployment. The backend API and database are not currently implemented.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E ( The two approaches share the same frontend pattern: because the React UI already loads information through the Fetch API, moving from local JSON to a real bus-management API mainly changes the data source rather than the interface design.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
82 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E ( College students often depend on campus buses for daily transportation, but information about bus routes, timings, available buses, and pass status is not always easy to access. Students may have to manually check schedules or contact transport staff to know which bus to take, whether a bus is available, or whether their bus pass has been approved. This creates the core problem addressed in this case study: how should a web application be designed so that students can easily view bus information, check routes and timings, apply for a bus pass, and track their pass status through a single interface?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (This topic is important in full stack development because it involves user input handling and validation, data retrieval and separation of data from presentation, and responsive frontend state management. The important challenge is connecting user actions, application data, state, and UI updates into one coherent working flow. The same pattern can be applied to other transportation and campus-management applications. The proposed system provides students with a centralized platform to view bus routes, timings, available buses, driver information, and submit bus pass applications. The current version is designed as a frontend prototype using simulated JSON data, while a real backend and database can be added in future development.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
